WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.025
Now Azure.

2
00:00:01.025 --> 00:00:05.999
The workbook's DNS, DHCP and IPAM
book covers the Private Resolver's

3
00:00:05.999 --> 00:00:10.450
hub-and-spoke design, so here is
the summary with today's limits.

4
00:00:10.450 --> 00:00:14.970
The inbound endpoint receives queries
from on premises or other clouds

5
00:00:14.970 --> 00:00:19.038
and answers from private DNS zones
linked to its virtual network.

6
00:00:19.038 --> 00:00:23.438
There can be five per resolver, at
10,000 queries per second each.

7
00:00:23.438 --> 00:00:29.006
The outbound endpoint and its forwarding
ruleset send queries for named domains to

8
00:00:29.006 --> 00:00:32.108
target servers; a ruleset
holds up to 1,000 rules,

9
00:00:32.108 --> 00:00:35.051
and a virtual network
links only one ruleset.

10
00:00:35.051 --> 00:00:38.891
It's a single-region service,
zone-redundant inside the region,

11
00:00:38.891 --> 00:00:44.087
and it doesn't work in encrypted virtual
networks or IPv6-enabled subnets.

12
00:00:44.087 --> 00:00:47.192
And Azure DNS doesn't
support zone transfers,

13
00:00:47.192 --> 00:00:52.370
so a BIND server can't be a secondary of
an Azure private zone, or the reverse.
